2. The Problem
Different devices used different chips and operating systems.
C and C++ programs had to be recompiled or rewritten for different platforms.
Software compatibility was a major problem.
Developers wanted a common programming language that could work on different devices.
3. The Idea: Write Once, Run Anywhere
Java was created to solve this problem.
Write the program once.
Compile it into bytecode.
Run the same bytecode on different platforms using the JVM (Java Virtual Machine).
Example:
A Java program can run on:
Windows
macOS
Linux
Main Goal: Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA).

How Java Evolved: 30 Years of Steady Upgrades
Java has evolved over the years by introducing new features, improving performance, and making programming easier.
1. Java 1.0 (1996)
First official release of Java.
Introduced JVM, core libraries and applets.
Introduced the concept of Write Once, Run Anywhere.
2. Java 5 (2004)
Generics
Annotations
Enums
Enhanced for-loop
3. Java 8 (2014)
Lambda expressions
Stream API
New Date and Time API
Functional programming support
4. Java 11 (2018)
First LTS release after Java 8.
Standard HTTP Client.
Improved performance and security.
5. Java 17 (2021)
Records
Sealed classes
Pattern matching improvements
Text blocks
6. Java 21 (2023)
Virtual threads
Pattern matching for switch
Record patterns
Easier concurrent programming
7. Java 25 (2025)
Compact source files.
Instance main methods.
Compact object headers.
Improved developer productivity.

Important: What is LTS?
LTS = Long-Term Support
LTS versions receive long-term updates, security fixes and support.
